# release.yml builds the one tarball every channel publishes with `upm pack`, which follows this
# file as `npm pack` would and adds only the signature, so these rules decide what consumers
# receive. OpenUPM publishes that tarball from the GitHub Release as-is.
#
# A deny list rather than a package.json "files" allow list: Unity needs a .meta file beside
# every shipped asset, and an allow list that misses one produces a package that imports with
# regenerated GUIDs and broken references — a failure that is invisible until someone installs it.

# Tests/ is deliberately NOT excluded, though it is the bulk of the file count. Shipping tests is
# conventional for a Unity package, and the asmdefs are gated on UNITY_INCLUDE_TESTS, so they do
# not compile in a consumer's project unless they opt in by listing the package under `testables`.
# They also make the contract's edge cases readable to anyone deciding whether to trust it.

# The analyzer's source, unit tests and staleness probe. Consumers need only the built assembly
# at Runtime/Analyzers/ERS.Singleton.Analyzers.dll, which ships. Shipping the sources would also
# drop three .csproj files into a consumer's package cache for their IDE to discover.
Analyzers~/
